1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,490
Carbon has four valence electrons,
and it makes four bonds.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:09,422
But look closer at where those four
electrons actually sit, and carbon looks

3
00:00:09,472 --> 00:00:11,410
like it should only make two.

4
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:14,318
Yet methane has four.

5
00:00:14,368 --> 00:00:19,710
Four identical bonds, pointing to the
corners of a tetrahedron, a little

6
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:24,116
triangular pyramid. So we're missing
something about where electrons live.

7
00:00:24,166 --> 00:00:26,366
That's what orbitals are about.

8
00:00:26,866 --> 00:00:31,456
In this video you'll learn to write
the electron configuration of any atom

9
00:00:31,506 --> 00:00:35,906
up to calcium, and draw
its electrons box by box.

10
00:00:35,956 --> 00:00:39,466
And you'll see why carbon
seems stuck at two bonds.

11
00:00:39,966 --> 00:00:43,425
Orbitals usually get taught as weird
quantum stuff you just have to accept.

12
00:00:43,475 --> 00:00:45,292
But you don't need the math.

13
00:00:45,342 --> 00:00:47,136
You need one picture.

14
00:00:47,186 --> 00:00:52,396
An orbital is a space around the
nucleus where an electron is likely to

15
00:00:52,446 --> 00:00:55,790
be, and each one holds two electrons.

16
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,806
Almost everything in this
video falls out of that.

17
00:00:59,306 --> 00:01:01,809
Quick recap from last time.

18
00:01:01,859 --> 00:01:03,949
Electrons sit in shells.

19
00:01:03,999 --> 00:01:06,549
The inner one, K, holds two.

20
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:09,309
The next one, L, holds eight.

21
00:01:09,359 --> 00:01:13,522
The outer ones, the valence
electrons, do the bonding.

22
00:01:13,572 --> 00:01:16,002
We never said why two and eight.

23
00:01:16,052 --> 00:01:16,999
We will.

24
00:01:20,049 --> 00:01:22,366
Start with the simplest atom.

25
00:01:22,416 --> 00:01:26,572
Hydrogen. One proton in
the middle, one electron.

26
00:01:26,622 --> 00:01:31,739
Those shell rings from last time were
a map of energy levels, not tracks.

27
00:01:31,789 --> 00:01:34,759
The electron doesn't circle
the nucleus like a planet.

28
00:01:34,809 --> 00:01:37,769
Measure where it is,
and you get a snapshot.

29
00:01:37,819 --> 00:01:40,499
Measure again, somewhere else.

30
00:01:40,549 --> 00:01:44,649
And again. Every snapshot
finds it somewhere new.

31
00:01:45,149 --> 00:01:49,711
Mark each sighting with a dot, and
keep going for thousands of them.

32
00:01:49,761 --> 00:01:52,024
The dots build a cloud.

33
00:01:52,074 --> 00:01:57,199
Thick near the nucleus, and
thinner the farther out you go.

34
00:01:57,249 --> 00:02:00,129
So you can't say where the electron is.

35
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:02,027
Only where it's likely to be.

36
00:02:02,527 --> 00:02:06,893
The cloud never really ends,
so chemists pick a cutoff.

37
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:11,405
Draw a boundary around the space
where the electron turns up 90% of the

38
00:02:11,455 --> 00:02:15,157
time. That space is an orbital.

39
00:02:15,207 --> 00:02:18,104
A cloud of chances, not a path.

40
00:02:18,154 --> 00:02:21,317
Hydrogen's electron is in one called 1s.

41
00:02:21,367 --> 00:02:26,205
The one is the shell, the first shell, K.

42
00:02:26,255 --> 00:02:29,077
Chemists call that number n.

43
00:02:29,127 --> 00:02:33,161
The s is the shape, and
s orbitals are round.

44
00:02:36,211 --> 00:02:38,785
Next, helium. Two electrons.

45
00:02:38,835 --> 00:02:40,461
Both fit in that same 1s orbital.

46
00:02:40,511 --> 00:02:41,657
But they're not identical.

47
00:02:41,707 --> 00:02:43,809
They differ in something called spin.

48
00:02:43,859 --> 00:02:49,821
Each electron acts like a tiny magnet
pointing one of two ways, up or down.

49
00:02:49,871 --> 00:02:52,374
And helium's two point opposite ways.

50
00:02:52,424 --> 00:02:55,211
We draw them as an up
arrow and a down arrow.

51
00:02:55,711 --> 00:02:58,401
Now lithium, three electrons.

52
00:02:58,451 --> 00:03:00,621
The third one can't squeeze into 1s.

53
00:03:00,671 --> 00:03:02,717
That's called the Pauli
exclusion principle.

54
00:03:02,767 --> 00:03:06,501
You can't really ask why on this
one, it's just how electrons are.

55
00:03:06,551 --> 00:03:11,821
No two electrons in an atom can have
the same orbital and the same spin.

56
00:03:11,871 --> 00:03:15,821
There are only two spins, so an
orbital holds two electrons, max.

57
00:03:15,871 --> 00:03:21,101
One up, one down. And shell one has
only that one orbital, so lithium's

58
00:03:21,151 --> 00:03:23,471
third electron has to start shell two.

59
00:03:23,971 --> 00:03:27,718
Quick check. Could an orbital
ever hold three electrons?

60
00:03:31,218 --> 00:03:34,755
Never. Only two spins, so two electrons.

61
00:03:34,805 --> 00:03:39,075
A third would match one of the others
in both orbital and spin, and Pauli

62
00:03:39,125 --> 00:03:40,045
doesn't allow that.

63
00:03:43,095 --> 00:03:44,465
So what's in shell two?

64
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:49,685
One thing first. Electrons behave a
lot like waves, and a wave can have

65
00:03:49,735 --> 00:03:51,618
spots where it's exactly zero.

66
00:03:51,668 --> 00:03:54,345
Those spots are called nodes.

67
00:03:54,395 --> 00:03:57,485
Shell two has its own s orbital, 2s.

68
00:03:57,535 --> 00:04:02,432
Same round shape, just bigger, because
an electron in shell two spends most

69
00:04:02,482 --> 00:04:03,985
of its time farther out.

70
00:04:04,035 --> 00:04:08,978
And hidden inside it is a node, a small
sphere where the electron is never

71
00:04:09,028 --> 00:04:11,725
found. It's not a wall
it has to climb over.

72
00:04:11,775 --> 00:04:13,695
The wave is just zero there.

73
00:04:14,195 --> 00:04:16,922
Shell two also brings a new shape.

74
00:04:16,972 --> 00:04:22,124
The p orbital. Two lobes, on opposite
sides of the nucleus, along the x

75
00:04:22,174 --> 00:04:24,987
axis. People call it a dumbbell.

76
00:04:25,037 --> 00:04:28,625
Between the lobes is a flat
sheet called a nodal plane.

77
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:33,037
It runs right through the nucleus,
and the electron is never found on it.

78
00:04:33,087 --> 00:04:35,185
And this one's a really common mix up.

79
00:04:35,235 --> 00:04:39,159
Those two lobes are one orbital, not two.

80
00:04:39,209 --> 00:04:42,422
So the whole thing still
holds just two electrons.

81
00:04:42,912 --> 00:04:44,612
And there are three of them.

82
00:04:44,662 --> 00:04:47,799
2px, along x. 2py, along y.

83
00:04:47,849 --> 00:04:51,632
And 2pz, pointing straight out at you.

84
00:04:51,682 --> 00:04:54,632
Each one sits at 90° to the other two.

85
00:04:54,682 --> 00:04:57,220
They're the same shape
and the same energy.

86
00:04:57,270 --> 00:04:59,092
They just point different ways.

87
00:04:59,142 --> 00:05:04,542
And when orbitals have exactly the same
energy, chemists call them degenerate.

88
00:05:05,042 --> 00:05:10,629
Now wait a second. If carbon bonds
using orbitals at 90°, its bonds should

89
00:05:10,679 --> 00:05:14,345
be 90° apart. But methane's
angles are 109.5°.

90
00:05:14,395 --> 00:05:16,641
Keep that in your back pocket.

91
00:05:16,691 --> 00:05:18,422
It's a big clue.

92
00:05:18,922 --> 00:05:21,298
There's a pattern to which shapes show up.

93
00:05:21,348 --> 00:05:23,405
Shell one only gets s.

94
00:05:23,455 --> 00:05:30,312
Shell two adds p. Shell three adds five
d orbitals, mostly four leaf clovers.

95
00:05:30,362 --> 00:05:33,105
Shell four adds seven f's.

96
00:05:33,155 --> 00:05:37,135
But organic chemistry lives
almost entirely in s and p.

97
00:05:40,185 --> 00:05:42,279
Okay, now we can answer the
question from last time.

98
00:05:42,329 --> 00:05:45,265
Shell one has just one orbital.

99
00:05:45,315 --> 00:05:47,628
Two electrons each, so it holds two.

100
00:05:47,678 --> 00:05:51,175
Shell two has 2s plus three p's.

101
00:05:51,225 --> 00:05:55,055
Four orbitals. Four times two...

102
00:05:55,105 --> 00:05:57,825
eight. That's where the two
and the eight come from.

103
00:05:58,325 --> 00:06:00,726
And that's what's behind the octet rule.

104
00:06:00,776 --> 00:06:05,675
Carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine
bond with shell two, which has exactly

105
00:06:05,725 --> 00:06:10,115
four orbitals. That's eight electrons,
and there's no room for a ninth.

106
00:06:10,165 --> 00:06:13,942
A ninth would have to go up into a
higher shell, farther out and way higher

107
00:06:13,992 --> 00:06:18,942
in energy. So a full shell two is a
really low energy arrangement, and these

108
00:06:18,992 --> 00:06:21,765
atoms share or gain
electrons to get there.

109
00:06:22,265 --> 00:06:27,028
Quick check. How many electrons can
the whole set of three p orbitals in

110
00:06:27,078 --> 00:06:28,318
one shell hold?

111
00:06:31,818 --> 00:06:34,015
Three orbitals, two each.

112
00:06:34,065 --> 00:06:39,045
Six. If you said two, you counted
one p orbital instead of the set.

113
00:06:42,095 --> 00:06:45,281
You've probably noticed the p
orbital is drawn in two colors.

114
00:06:45,331 --> 00:06:49,285
Remember, the electron behaves like
a wave, and a wave has a part above

115
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:51,285
the line and a part below.

116
00:06:51,335 --> 00:06:53,917
A plus part and a minus part.

117
00:06:53,967 --> 00:06:58,178
That sign is called the phase,
and the two colors just mark it.

118
00:06:58,228 --> 00:07:02,685
It flips right at the nodal plane,
which is why the wave is zero there.

119
00:07:02,735 --> 00:07:04,770
And they're not charges.

120
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:07,575
Neither lobe is positive
or negative like a proton.

121
00:07:08,075 --> 00:07:12,878
So why care? Because phase decides
what happens when two atoms' orbitals

122
00:07:12,928 --> 00:07:17,938
overlap. Say two hydrogens come together,
and their 1s orbitals are in the

123
00:07:17,988 --> 00:07:22,178
same phase. The waves add
up between the nuclei.

124
00:07:22,228 --> 00:07:26,764
The electron pair gathers right there,
and both positive nuclei get pulled

125
00:07:26,814 --> 00:07:28,878
toward that negative pair in the middle.

126
00:07:28,928 --> 00:07:29,848
That's a bond.

127
00:07:30,348 --> 00:07:33,138
Now flip one. Opposite phase.

128
00:07:33,188 --> 00:07:38,091
The waves cancel in the middle, and a
node shows up right between the nuclei.

129
00:07:38,141 --> 00:07:41,465
So the electrons get pushed out of
exactly the spot that would hold the

130
00:07:41,515 --> 00:07:47,008
atoms together. We call that
antibonding, and it means no bond.

131
00:07:47,058 --> 00:07:48,651
You can park phase for now.

132
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:50,368
It comes back with double bonds.

133
00:07:53,418 --> 00:07:55,548
So which orbitals fill first?

134
00:07:55,598 --> 00:07:57,868
The lowest energy ones.

135
00:07:57,918 --> 00:08:01,254
That's the Aufbau principle,
German for building up.

136
00:08:01,304 --> 00:08:05,272
And inside a shell, s is lower than p.

137
00:08:05,322 --> 00:08:06,948
Boron, five electrons.

138
00:08:06,998 --> 00:08:09,888
Two in 1s. Two in 2s.

139
00:08:09,938 --> 00:08:12,098
Only the fifth goes into 2p.

140
00:08:12,598 --> 00:08:15,588
We write that as an
electron configuration.

141
00:08:15,638 --> 00:08:19,934
You write the orbital label, then a
little raised number that says how many

142
00:08:19,984 --> 00:08:26,081
electrons are in it. So
boron is 1s², 2s², 2p¹.

143
00:08:26,131 --> 00:08:30,537
The raised numbers should
add up to the electron count.

144
00:08:30,587 --> 00:08:32,211
Five. Good.

145
00:08:32,711 --> 00:08:35,181
But why is s lower than p?

146
00:08:35,231 --> 00:08:41,151
The 1s pair sits close in and partly
blocks the nucleus's pull on anything

147
00:08:41,201 --> 00:08:43,997
farther out. That's shielding.

148
00:08:44,047 --> 00:08:48,573
This graph shows how likely an
electron is at each distance.

149
00:08:48,623 --> 00:08:51,261
See that little 2s bump
inside the 1s hump?

150
00:08:51,311 --> 00:08:54,050
It dives in past the shield.

151
00:08:54,100 --> 00:09:00,501
A 2p electron gets in less, and it's
never right at the nucleus, because

152
00:09:00,551 --> 00:09:02,541
the nucleus sits on its nodal plane.

153
00:09:02,591 --> 00:09:06,111
That diving in has a name.

154
00:09:06,161 --> 00:09:12,050
Penetration. More penetration means
a stronger pull, and that means lower

155
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:12,900
energy.

156
00:09:13,131 --> 00:09:16,121
Let's do one together.

157
00:09:16,171 --> 00:09:18,261
Phosphorus, fifteen electrons.

158
00:09:18,311 --> 00:09:21,541
1s takes two. 2s takes two.

159
00:09:21,591 --> 00:09:25,021
2p takes six. That's ten.

160
00:09:25,071 --> 00:09:28,541
3s, two more. Twelve.

161
00:09:28,591 --> 00:09:31,321
And 3p gets the last three.

162
00:09:31,371 --> 00:09:32,311
Fifteen.

163
00:09:32,811 --> 00:09:37,362
Your turn. Pause and write the
configuration for chlorine.

164
00:09:37,412 --> 00:09:40,355
Seventeen electrons.

165
00:09:45,855 --> 00:09:49,474
Same start as phosphorus, up through 3s².

166
00:09:49,524 --> 00:09:54,013
Twelve. Five left, so 3p⁵.

167
00:09:54,063 --> 00:09:54,935
That's it.

168
00:09:57,985 --> 00:09:59,485
Now the one that catches people.

169
00:09:59,535 --> 00:10:04,335
Potassium, symbol K. Not the
K shell, just a coincidence.

170
00:10:04,385 --> 00:10:08,367
Nineteen electrons, and the first
eighteen fill just like argon.

171
00:10:08,417 --> 00:10:10,388
So where does the nineteenth go?

172
00:10:10,438 --> 00:10:11,908
3d feels obvious, right?

173
00:10:11,958 --> 00:10:13,562
Three comes before four.

174
00:10:13,612 --> 00:10:15,225
But it goes into 4s.

175
00:10:15,713 --> 00:10:16,895
Same reason as before.

176
00:10:16,945 --> 00:10:22,255
Penetration. A 3d electron, like
p, is never right at the nucleus.

177
00:10:22,305 --> 00:10:25,295
A 4s electron dives in close a lot.

178
00:10:25,345 --> 00:10:26,868
That's enough to put 4s lower.

179
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:29,155
So energy sets the order.

180
00:10:29,205 --> 00:10:30,345
Not the shell number.

181
00:10:30,845 --> 00:10:34,652
These get long, so chemists
shorten the filled part.

182
00:10:34,702 --> 00:10:39,975
Argon's symbol in brackets stands for
all eighteen of argon's electrons.

183
00:10:40,025 --> 00:10:44,135
So potassium is argon
in brackets, then 4s¹.

184
00:10:44,185 --> 00:10:46,925
If you wrote 3d¹, that's the trap.

185
00:10:47,425 --> 00:10:50,436
Your turn. Calcium, twenty electrons.

186
00:10:50,486 --> 00:10:52,393
Pause and write it the short way.

187
00:10:56,893 --> 00:11:02,069
Eighteen are argon. Two
left, and 4s holds two.

188
00:11:02,119 --> 00:11:04,683
Argon in brackets, 4s².

189
00:11:04,733 --> 00:11:06,986
3d only starts after that.

190
00:11:10,036 --> 00:11:14,906
One more rule, and it's the one
that ties all this to bonding.

191
00:11:14,956 --> 00:11:17,206
Nitrogen, seven electrons.

192
00:11:17,256 --> 00:11:20,146
1s² 2s², and three left for 2p.

193
00:11:20,196 --> 00:11:23,066
Draw each orbital as a box.

194
00:11:23,116 --> 00:11:25,566
Do they go in as a pair plus one?

195
00:11:25,616 --> 00:11:28,866
No. One goes into each box.

196
00:11:28,916 --> 00:11:30,716
All pointing the same way.

197
00:11:31,216 --> 00:11:35,932
That's Hund's rule. Orbitals with
the same energy get one electron each

198
00:11:35,982 --> 00:11:37,546
before any of them pair up.

199
00:11:37,596 --> 00:11:42,899
Why? Two electrons in one box share the
same space, and they're both negative,

200
00:11:42,949 --> 00:11:44,419
so they shove each other.

201
00:11:44,469 --> 00:11:46,866
An empty box lets them stay apart.

202
00:11:46,916 --> 00:11:50,419
Why they all point the same way takes
quantum mechanics we're skipping,

203
00:11:50,469 --> 00:11:52,073
so take that part as given.

204
00:11:52,123 --> 00:11:55,736
An electron alone in its
box is called unpaired.

205
00:11:56,236 --> 00:11:57,586
And this is the cool part.

206
00:11:57,636 --> 00:11:59,978
Count nitrogen's unpaired electrons.

207
00:12:00,028 --> 00:12:06,146
Three. Each one pairs up with an
electron from a hydrogen, and each shared

208
00:12:06,196 --> 00:12:08,866
pair is a bond. Three bonds.

209
00:12:08,916 --> 00:12:13,116
And the pair nitrogen already had
shows up as ammonia's lone pair.

210
00:12:13,616 --> 00:12:15,986
Oxygen, eight electrons.

211
00:12:16,036 --> 00:12:19,150
The first three 2p
electrons take a box each.

212
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:21,166
The fourth has to pair up.

213
00:12:21,216 --> 00:12:25,166
So two unpaired, and
oxygen makes two bonds.

214
00:12:25,216 --> 00:12:29,576
And its two pairs in shell two
show up as its two lone pairs.

215
00:12:30,076 --> 00:12:33,933
Your turn. Fluorine, nine electrons.

216
00:12:33,983 --> 00:12:37,476
Pause, fill the boxes, and
count the unpaired ones.

217
00:12:42,976 --> 00:12:47,070
Five go into 2p. One per
box, then two of them pair.

218
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:50,230
One unpaired, so one bond.

219
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,365
And three lone pairs.

220
00:12:52,415 --> 00:12:53,880
Exactly what fluorine does.

221
00:12:54,380 --> 00:12:57,310
So now try carbon. Six electrons.

222
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:02,670
1s² 2s² 2p². Hund puts
those two in separate boxes.

223
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:04,850
Two unpaired, one empty box.

224
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,162
So carbon should make two bonds.

225
00:13:08,212 --> 00:13:13,970
Something like CH₂. But it makes four,
in basically every stable organic

226
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:16,274
molecule you'll ever draw.

227
00:13:16,324 --> 00:13:20,143
So carbon's electrons have
to rearrange before it bonds.

228
00:13:20,193 --> 00:13:23,180
And that's the whole next video.

229
00:13:26,230 --> 00:13:28,640
Last thing, and it
saves a lot of counting.

230
00:13:28,690 --> 00:13:33,420
The periodic table is sorted by which
orbital the last electron goes into.

231
00:13:33,470 --> 00:13:36,400
The two columns on the
left are the s block.

232
00:13:36,450 --> 00:13:39,160
Two, because an s orbital holds two.

233
00:13:39,210 --> 00:13:42,100
The six on the right are the p block.

234
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:44,820
Six, because three p's hold six.

235
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:49,209
The ten in the middle are the d block,
and the two rows hanging off the

236
00:13:49,259 --> 00:13:50,950
bottom are the f block.

237
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,327
Besides hydrogen, pretty much every
element you'll use in organic chemistry

238
00:13:55,377 --> 00:13:56,630
is in the p block.

239
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:04,777
So, in one picture. An orbital is a
space where an electron is likely to

240
00:14:04,827 --> 00:14:09,043
be. It holds two, with opposite spins.

241
00:14:09,093 --> 00:14:15,047
S orbitals are spheres, and p orbitals
are dumbbells, three per shell, at

242
00:14:15,097 --> 00:14:17,190
right angles to each other.

243
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:23,767
Shell two has four orbitals, so it
holds eight, and that's your octet.

244
00:14:23,817 --> 00:14:28,910
Lowest energy fills first,
and 4s comes before 3d.

245
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:33,883
Hund spreads them out before pairing,
and the unpaired ones predict the

246
00:14:33,933 --> 00:14:36,520
bonds. Except for carbon.

247
00:14:39,570 --> 00:14:44,430
Alright, practice. Pause, try each
one, then I'll walk through it.

248
00:14:44,480 --> 00:14:48,653
Problem one. Sulfur, sixteen electrons.

249
00:14:48,703 --> 00:14:52,730
Write its configuration, and say
how many unpaired electrons it has.

250
00:14:59,230 --> 00:15:01,580
Up through 3s² is twelve.

251
00:15:01,630 --> 00:15:05,800
Four left, so 3p⁴. Now the boxes.

252
00:15:05,850 --> 00:15:09,153
One in each, then the fourth pairs up.

253
00:15:09,203 --> 00:15:15,706
Two unpaired. The trap is pairing
them two and two, and getting zero.

254
00:15:15,756 --> 00:15:17,830
Always spread out first.

255
00:15:18,330 --> 00:15:23,965
Problem two. Two hydrogen 1s
orbitals overlap, in opposite colors.

256
00:15:24,015 --> 00:15:28,531
A student says opposite colors
attract, so it's a strong bond.

257
00:15:28,581 --> 00:15:29,461
Right or wrong?

258
00:15:34,961 --> 00:15:40,404
Wrong. The colors are the
phase of the wave, not charges.

259
00:15:40,454 --> 00:15:44,951
Opposite phase means the waves cancel
between the nuclei, so there's a node

260
00:15:45,001 --> 00:15:47,737
right in the middle. Antibonding.

261
00:15:47,787 --> 00:15:52,494
No bond. It's matching colors,
the same phase, that make a bond.

262
00:15:55,544 --> 00:16:00,494
And that's orbitals. The notes and practice
for this topic are free on levlprep.com.

263
00:16:00,544 --> 00:16:02,134
Next up, hybridization.

264
00:16:02,184 --> 00:16:10,074
How carbon turns two unpaired
electrons into four identical bonds.

265
00:16:10,124 --> 00:16:10,924
See you there.
